---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import CardGroup from '../components/CardGroup.astro';
import ActivityCard from '../components/ActivityCard.astro';
import StepList from '../components/StepList.astro';
import Step from '../components/Step.astro';
import PhotoFrame from '../components/PhotoFrame.astro';
import FaqItem from '../components/FaqItem.astro';

// Copy: rewritten from content-source/waypoint-content-v1.md, section "5 For Young People".
// Wes's build notes: written to young people aged 8–16 (mainly 9–13), not babyish. Lead with
// projects, teamwork and having a say; never define young people by behaviour. No referral
// form or direct child data collection, so the hero has no Get in touch button.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Hands-on projects for young people"
  description="Build, invent and test things with other young people at Waypoint in Shoreham-by-Sea, for ages 8–16."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Hands-on projects for young people"
    strapline="Build, invent and test things with other young people."
    hideCta
  />

  <Section
    id="try-ideas"
    title="What Waypoint is"
    strapline="Could you build a basecamp that really works?"
    drawing="chain-reaction"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of a ball rolling down a ramp into a row of dominoes"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Could you set off a giant chain reaction, and work out how to make it even longer? Waypoint is about trying
          ideas for real. You'll build, invent, test things and work with other young people. You might be the one with
          the design, the builder, the tester or the person who spots a better way.
        </p>
        <p>
          You don't have to be sporty, know anyone already or have all the answers. Just bring your curiosity, and we'll
          help you find a way to join in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section id="projects" title="Projects" strapline="What could you get stuck into?">
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Here are some of the projects we're working on. They're examples, not a fixed timetable. Adults plan and lead
          them, and you get to suggest ideas and make choices. Some happen indoors and some outside.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <CardGroup columns={2}>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Build the Basecamp"
        drawing="basecamp"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a tent with a flag between two pine trees"
      >
        Plan and build a base using ropes and other materials. Decide what it needs, work with your group and see how
        your design holds up.
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="The Giant Chain Reaction"
        drawing="chain-reaction"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a ball rolling down a ramp into a row of dominoes"
      >
        Link ramps, balls and everyday objects into one long sequence. Change the design, test it and see what your
        group can make happen.
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Survival Village"
        drawing="survival-village"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a small village of huts, a tipi with a flag and a campfire"
      >
        Imagine a settlement and plan what your group needs. Who builds what? What will you do with the materials
        you've got?
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Waypoint Game Studio"
        drawing="game-studio"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of two stick people pointing at a screen showing a simple game"
      >
        Invent characters, rules and levels for your own game idea, then work with an adult to turn it into a simple
        game you can test.
      </ActivityCard>
    </CardGroup>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="your-strengths"
    title="Your strengths"
    strapline="Everyone brings something different."
    drawing="building"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of two stick people carrying a plank, with a toolbox"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Some people love coming up with ideas. Others like building, working out how things work, organising the
          materials or testing what the group has made. There's room for all of it, and plenty of chances to try
          something new.
        </p>
        <p>
          You might come to Waypoint through your school or through home education. You don't have to explain why
          you're here to anyone else. It's all about what you get to do together.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section id="meet-wes" title="Meet Wes" strapline="Hi, I'm Wes.">
    <PhotoFrame
      slot="photo"
      src="/images/wes-horscroft-480.webp"
      srcset="/images/wes-horscroft-480.webp 480w, /images/wes-horscroft-960.webp 960w"
      sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 18rem, 16rem"
      alt="Wes Horscroft, founder of Waypoint"
      width={480}
      height={588}
      ratio="4 / 5"
      position="50% 30%"
      side="left"
    />
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I've been teaching PE and coaching young people since 2007, I'm Director of Rugby at a school in Hove, and
          I've raced for Great Britain in Hawaii. I love seeing what happens when someone has an idea and the group
          works out how to make it real.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'll show you around, explain what we're doing and help you find a way to get involved. I'd like to know what
          you're into and what you'd like to try. You can tell me yourself, show me an idea or ask someone you trust to
          help explain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="your-first-day"
    title="Your first day"
    strapline="What happens when you arrive."
    drawing="day"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of the sun's path across the sky, with five stops along the way"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You won't have to make a speech about yourself or know how everything works on day one. You'll get to know the
          place while doing something practical.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="steps">
      <StepList>
        <Step title="Before you come">
          <p>
            We'll tell you who you'll meet and what the day will look like. You can tell us what you're looking forward
            to and ask questions in whatever way suits you.
          </p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="When you arrive">
          <p>
            We'll welcome you and show you the main space, where to put your things and where the toilets are. You'll
            know who to ask if you want to check something.
          </p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Get started">
          <p>
            We'll show you the challenge and how to begin. You might watch a demonstration, pick a part to work on or
            get stuck into building with others.
          </p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Breaks and home time">
          <p>There are breaks and lunch, and you'll know the plan for going home.</p>
        </Step>
      </StepList>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="good-questions"
    title="Good questions"
    strapline="Things people often ask."
    drawing="idea"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of a stick person with a lightbulb idea, sketching at an easel"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Tap a question to see the answer.</p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="faqs">
      <FaqItem question="What if I don't know anyone yet?">
        <p>
          That's fine. We'll introduce you to your group and help everyone get involved. Working on a project together
          gives you something to talk about and do.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Do I have to like sport?">
        <p>
          Not at all. There are loads of ways to get involved, whether you like designing, making, solving puzzles,
          organising or testing ideas.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Can I get help with something?">
        <p>Of course. An adult can show you how something works, help you try a first step or work it out with you.</p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Will there be breaks?">
        <p>
          Yes, there are breaks and lunch. If you need a pause during an activity, ask an adult and they'll help you take
          a supervised break.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Can I bring my own ideas?">
        <p>
          Yes. Adults plan the activities and keep everyone safe, but you can suggest designs, share ideas and help decide
          how your group tackles a challenge. You can explain an idea, draw it or show someone.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Who will hear about what I've been doing?">
        <p>
          We'll explain this before you start. If you come through school, we'll share useful updates with your school
          and the people at home. If you're home educated, we'll agree with your family how to share updates. We'd like
          to include what you think about your day too.
        </p>
        <p>
          If we're ever worried about your safety or someone else's, we may need to tell an adult who can help. We'll
          explain what we need to do.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
      <FaqItem question="Who can I talk to if I have a question or a worry?">
        <p>
          You can talk to Wes, a parent or carer, someone at school or another adult you trust. You can ask someone else
          to help you explain.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the worry is about someone at Waypoint, you can tell a trusted adult outside Waypoint. You don't have to
          speak to that person first.
        </p>
      </FaqItem>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="want-to-try"
    title="Want to try Waypoint?"
    strapline="Show this page to someone at home or school."
    brand
    drawing="chat"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of two stick people chatting, with speech bubbles"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Have a look at the projects and see what catches your eye. Then show this page to your parent, carer or someone
          at school, and they can get in touch to find out more.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="route-links">
        <li><a href="/about/#activities">See all the activities</a></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="button-row">
        <Button href="/parents-and-carers/" variant="accent">Information for your parent or carer</Button>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .steps,
  .faqs {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .route-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .route-links a::after {
    content: ' \2192';
  }
</style>
